Experiment 1: Linux Basics, Redirection & Process Management
Directory & File Operations: Essential command syntax and options for navigating (pwd, cd), managing directories (mkdir -p/-v/-m, rmdir -p/-v), listing metadata (ls -lah), creating/updating files (touch -c/-t/-d), copying (cp -r/-i/-p), moving/renaming (mv -i/-n/-v), deleting (rm -rf/-i), viewing contents (cat -n/-b), and extracting line ranges (head, tail).
I/O Redirection & Streams: Understanding standard streams (stdin 0, stdout 1, stderr 2), overwriting (>) vs. appending (>>), redirecting errors (2>), combining output and error streams (&>), and feeding file input (<).
Pipes & Utilities: Stream chaining via pipes (|), alphabetical sorting (sort), deduplication (uniq), splitting output streams (tee), and converting stream inputs to command-line arguments (xargs).
Pattern Searching (grep) & Finding Files (find): Text search flags (-i, -v, -c, -w, -n) alongside disk-wide searches filtering by size (-size), name/case (-name, -iname), user ownership (-user), and empty paths (-empty).
Terminal Text Editors: Workflow modes, search, line management, and exit operations in modal (vim) and modeless (nano) environments.
Process Management: Shell job control (jobs, bg, fg), process inspection (ps, ps -e, ps -ef, ps -p, ps aux), signal handling (kill -15 SIGTERM, kill -9 SIGKILL, kill -STOP, kill -CONT), CPU priority scheduling (nice, renice), and live diagnostic monitoring (top).
Theory:
Directory Operations
pwd (Print Working Directory)
● Description: Prints the full absolute path from the root directory to the current working
location.
● Syntax: pwd [OPTION]
● Example: pwd
● Output: /home/ubuntu
cd (Change Directory)
● Description: Navigates the terminal session to a different directory in the filesystem.
● Syntax: cd [DIRECTORY]
● System Example: cd /etc/netplan
● Key Shortcuts:
○ Move up one level: cd ..
○ Previous directory: cd -
○ Return to Home Directory (using shortcut): cd ~ (or simply cd)
○ Return to Home Directory (without shortcut): cd /home/ubuntu (or cd $HOME)

mkdir (Make Directory)
● Description: Creates one or more new directories in the filesystem.
● Syntax: mkdir [OPTION] DIRECTORY...
● Standard Creation:
○ Using ~: mkdir ~/scripts
○ Without ~: mkdir /home/ubuntu/scripts
● Useful Options:
○ -p (Parents): Creates intermediate directories automatically.
■ Using ~: mkdir -p ~/Documents/projects/app/src
■ Without ~: mkdir -p /home/ubuntu/Documents/projects/app/src
○ -v (Verbose): Prints confirmation for every created directory.
■ Using ~: mkdir -v ~/Downloads/iso

■ Without ~: mkdir -v /home/ubuntu/Downloads/iso
○ -m (Mode): Sets octal permissions upon creation.
■ Using ~: mkdir -m 700 ~/.ssh
■ Without ~: mkdir -m 700 /home/ubuntu/.ssh
● Modifying Permissions of an Existing Folder using chmod:
○ Using ~: chmod 755 ~/Documents/projects
○ Without ~: chmod 755 /home/ubuntu/Documents/projects

rmdir (Remove Directory)
● Description: Deletes empty directories safely.
● Syntax: rmdir [OPTION] DIRECTORY...
● Standard Removal:
○ Using ~: rmdir ~/Templates
○ Without ~: rmdir /home/ubuntu/Templates
● Useful Options:
○ -p (Parents): Removes empty directory chains.
■ Using ~: rmdir -p ~/Documents/projects/app/src
■ Without ~: rmdir -p /home/ubuntu/Documents/projects/app/src
○ -v (Verbose): Confirms deletion.
■ Using ~: rmdir -v ~/Downloads/iso
■ Without ~: rmdir -v /home/ubuntu/Downloads/iso

2. File Operations
ls (List Directory Contents)
● Description: Lists directory contents and metadata.
● Syntax: ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...
● Long Listing of Home (including hidden dotfiles):
○ Using ~: ls -lah ~
○ Without ~: ls -lah /home/ubuntu
● Useful Options:
○ -l (Long format): Displays file permissions, owner, group, size, and timestamp.
○ -a (All): Shows hidden configuration files (e.g., .bashrc, .profile).
○ -h (Human-readable): Renders file sizes in KB, MB, or GB.
○ -t (Time): Sorts contents by modification date, newest first.
○ -R (Recursive): Lists files across all nested subdirectories.

touch
● Description: Creates an empty file or updates the access/modification timestamps of an
existing file.
● Syntax: touch [OPTION]... FILE...
● Standard Creation:
○ Using ~: touch ~/.bash_aliases
○ Without ~: touch /home/ubuntu/.bash_aliases
● Useful Options:
○ -c (No create): Refreshes timestamp only if the file exists.
■ Using ~: touch -c ~/.bashrc
■ Without ~: touch -c /home/ubuntu/.bashrc
○ -t (Custom timestamp): Formats as [[CC]YY]MMDDhhmm[.ss].
■ Using ~: touch -t 202601011200 ~/install.log
■ Without ~: touch -t 202601011200 /home/ubuntu/install.log
○ -d (Date string): Accepts flexible human-readable dates.
■ Using ~: touch -d &quot;2026-04-23&quot; ~/release_notes.txt
■ Without ~: touch -d &quot;2026-04-23&quot; /home/ubuntu/release_notes.txt
■ Using ~: touch -d &quot;09:00&quot; ~/.bash_aliases
■ Without ~: touch -d &quot;09:00&quot; /home/ubuntu/.bash_aliases

cp (Copy)
● Description: Copies files and directories from source to destination.
● Syntax: cp [OPTION]... SOURCE... DEST
● Standard Copy:
○ Using ~: cp /etc/skel/.bashrc ~/.bashrc.bak
○ Without ~: cp /etc/skel/.bashrc /home/ubuntu/.bashrc.bak
● Useful Options:
○ -r / -R (Recursive): Copies full directory trees.
■ Using ~: cp -r /etc/netplan ~/backup_netplan
■ Without ~: cp -r /etc/netplan /home/ubuntu/backup_netplan
○ -i (Interactive): Prompts before overwriting existing files.
■ Using ~: cp -i /etc/hosts ~/hosts
■ Without ~: cp -i /etc/hosts /home/ubuntu/hosts
○ -p (Preserve): Retains modes, ownership, and timestamps.
■ Using ~: cp -p /etc/timezone ~/timezone_backup
■ Without ~: cp -p /etc/timezone /home/ubuntu/timezone_backup

mv (Move / Rename)

● Description: Moves files or directories to new locations or renames them.
● Syntax: mv [OPTION]... SOURCE... DEST
● Standard Move / Rename:
○ Using ~: mv ~/Desktop ~/Workspace
○ Without ~: mv /home/ubuntu/Desktop /home/ubuntu/Workspace
● Useful Options:
○ -i (Interactive): Prompts before overwriting destination files.
■ Using ~: mv -i ~/test.conf /etc/netplan/
■ Without ~: mv -i /home/ubuntu/test.conf /etc/netplan/
○ -n (No clobber): Prevents overwriting silently.
■ Using ~: mv -n ~/.bashrc.bak ~/.bashrc
■ Without ~: mv -n /home/ubuntu/.bashrc.bak /home/ubuntu/.bashrc
○ -v (Verbose): Prints names of moved items.
■ Using ~: mv -v ~/Downloads/*.deb ~/Packages/
■ Without ~: mv -v /home/ubuntu/Downloads/*.deb /home/ubuntu/Packages/

rm (Remove)
● Description: Permanently deletes files and directories.
● Syntax: rm [OPTION]... FILE...
● System Directory Example: rm -rf /tmp/apt-* (Targets global system temp folder; no ~
applicable)
● Useful Options:
○ -r (Recursive): Deletes directories and their contents.
■ Using ~: rm -r ~/Downloads/temp_setup/
■ Without ~: rm -r /home/ubuntu/Downloads/temp_setup/
○ -f (Force): Suppresses prompts and missing-file errors.
■ System path: rm -f /tmp/lockfile
○ -i (Interactive): Asks confirmation before each deletion.
■ Using ~: rm -i ~/.bash_history
■ Without ~: rm -i /home/ubuntu/.bash_history

cat (Concatenate)
● Description: Outputs entire file contents to standard output.
● Syntax: cat [OPTION]... [FILE]...
● System Example: cat /etc/os-release
● Useful Options:
○ -n (Number): Numbers every line of output.
■ Example: cat -n /etc/shells

○ -b (Number non-blank): Numbers non-empty lines only.
■ Example: cat -b /etc/issue

head &amp; tail
● Description: Displays the beginning or end of files and input streams.
● Syntax: head -n [N] FILE | tail -n [N] FILE
● System Inspection Examples:
○ First 10 user accounts: head -n 10 /etc/passwd
○ First 5 filesystem mounts: head -n 5 /etc/fstab
○ Last 25 auth logs: tail -n 25 /var/log/auth.log
○ Last 50 package install logs: tail -n 50 /var/log/dpkg.log
GREP (Global Regular Expression Print)
Terminal-based search tool functioning like Ctrl+F:
● Case 1 (Ignoring case sensitivity -i): grep -i &quot;error&quot; logs.txt

● Case 2 (Inverting matches -v): grep -v &quot;error&quot; logs.txt (excludes search terms)
● Case 3 (Counting lines matching a profession -c): grep -c &quot;Doctor&quot; users.csv
● Case 4 (Isolating exact words -w): grep -w &quot;Carly&quot; users.csv
● Case 5 (Pinpointing line numbers -n): grep -n &quot;Carly&quot; users.csv
